  body: "# US incapacity evidence and limited capacity\n\n## Authored interpretation\n\nThe source makes US incapacity
    a factual question for the DM using medical and lay evidence, treats medical opinion as non-binding, and distinguishes
    inability to work due to relevant loss of faculty from lack of local jobs.\n\n## Scope and exceptions\n\nThe
    limited-capacity provision is expressly not a rigid earnings rule and requires a broad view of circumstances.
    It belongs to retained US, not UC, ESA or an automated employability assessment. No medical conclusion or current
    earnings threshold is supplied.\n\n## Inspected passages\n\n- [DMG 72130, PDF page 7](https://assets.publishing.service.gov.uk/media/5a808824e5274a2e87dba585/dmgch72.pdf#page=7).\n\
    - [DMG 72135, PDF page 7](https://assets.publishing.service.gov.uk/media/5a808824e5274a2e87dba585/dmgch72.pdf#page=7).\n\
    - [DMG 72140, PDF page 8](https://assets.publishing.service.gov.uk/media/5a808824e5274a2e87dba585/dmgch72.pdf#page=8).\n\
    - [DMG 72141, PDF page 8](https://assets.publishing.service.gov.uk/media/5a808824e5274a2e87dba585/dmgch72.pdf#page=8).\n\
    \n## Evidence and review status\n\nIndependent experimental interpretation of captured DWP guidance, not an
    official DWP document. Definitions and semantic associations are model-assisted proposals awaiting specialist
    review. No individual entitlement decision, medical conclusion, award calculation or executable rule is provided.\n\
    \nSource observation, listing-page update, printed revision, legal event and claimant-event dates are separate.
    Current legal applicability is unresolved. Page numbers are one-based PDF positions; machine extraction may
    damage reading order, characters and footnotes. No visual PDF or specialist acceptance is claimed.\n\n- `dmg-vol11-ch72`:
    PDF `9a84638b670cf30857abd8f0b34b65529c5c3075dcd52198c66b50c343d54f3b`; extracted pages `2572584f692b21dde3ce3612396d6abc9e0a86f4c5b612a30ab54dd3f498feac`;
    acquired `2026-09-15T19:45:34.257115Z`.\n"
  resource: https://assets.publishing.service.gov.uk/media/5a808824e5274a2e87dba585/dmgch72.pdf#page=7
  semantic_relations:
  - target: term/dmg-decision-maker
    predicate: http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#related
    rationale: The source distinguishes the DM’s factual decision from medical opinion used as evidence.
    evidence:
    - page: page/dmg-vol11-ch72/0007
      quote: "72130   The question whether, as the result of the relevant loss of faculty, the claimant is\n   \
        \     incapable of work is a question of fact to be decided by the DM having regard to the\n        nature
        and degree of disablement, the medical evidence regarding incapacity and\n        any other relevant medical
        or lay evidence. The opinion of a medical adviser or of a\n        HCP regarding incapacity may be obtained1.
        Such an opinion is simply medical\n        evidence to help the authorities in reaching a decision and is
        not binding on them.\n        Departmental procedure provides for the opinion of members of a medical board
        to\n        be obtained automatically on certain occasions but, in addition, the DM can ask for\n      \
        \  a further opinion to be obtained at any time before his decision is given.\n                        \
        \                                                                     1 SS Act 98, s 19(2)\n\n\n       \
        \ Meaning of HCP"
      locator: DMG 72130
  - target: term/iidb-loss-of-faculty
    predicate: http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#related
    rationale: The US question is expressly connected to the relevant loss of faculty, with no claim of
      equivalence of benefit conditions.
    evidence:
    - page: page/dmg-vol11-ch72/0007
      quote: "72135   A person is incapable of work if, having regard to his age, education, experience,\n     \
        \   state of health and other personal factors, there is no work or type of work which he\n        can reasonably
        be expected to do1. The fact that there is no such work locally, or\n        that owing to the state of
        the labour market the claimant has only a remote prospect\n        of obtaining it, or that there is none
        in connection with the occupation in which they\n        have previously been employed, does not prove that
        he is incapable of work for US\n        purposes; the incapacity must be the result of the relevant loss
        of faculty and is not\n        concerned with inability to obtain work from other causes2.\n           \
        \                                                                        1 CI 99/48 (KL); 2 R(I) 43/54"
      locator: DMG 72135
